# Auditworthy — the runnable request catalog.
#
# Installed by /deliver:install-runbook. Fire these at a running instance from VS Code (REST
# Client) or a JetBrains IDE. Dev-auth headers mean there is no token setup — see RUNBOOK.md §3.
#
# WHEN YOU ADD AN ENDPOINT, ADD ITS REQUEST HERE IN THE SAME PR. A catalog that lags the code is
# how the next agent concludes a working endpoint is missing.
#
# @base — RUNBOOK.md Mode A (the default). The API port is PINNED in AppHost.cs, so 9433 is correct
# on every machine and nothing has to be pasted here. This used to say the port was dynamic and told
# you to take the endpoint off the Aspire dashboard; there was no such endpoint to take, and the API
# was really on the ASP.NET default :5000 (#79). Under Mode B it is whatever you passed to --urls
# (8094 there) — change the line below if you are running headless.
#
# Ports are NOT unique across the products on this machine (:8094 is shared), so a 200 from /alive
# can come from another product. `GET /api/platform/modules` must name `compliance`.

@base   = http://127.0.0.1:9433
@module = compliance

# Swap the role to assert a boundary: compliance-reader (reads only), compliance-analyst (may
# propose, may NOT approve), compliance-owner (may approve), system_admin (holds `*`).
#
# The @name and @email variables below — send them, and CHANGE THEM BOTH WHENEVER YOU CHANGE
# @subject (#64). (Spelled out because a line opening with `@name` is REST Client's request-naming
# directive, which this is not.) Dev-auth defaults the name claim to the constant "Dev User" and
# the email claim to "dev@plenipo.local" for EVERY subject, and the platform writes the persisted
# User.DisplayName and User.Email from those claims the first time a subject is seen and never
# again. Drop either header and two different people become one indistinguishable actor in the
# append-only audit and in the approvals queue — which is the one thing this product exists to make
# impossible. `.local` is reserved for local use, so these addresses can never be delivered to.
#
# It is first-contact-wins: once a subject has been provisioned as "Dev User", changing @name will
# NOT rename it (see RUNBOOK.md §3). Recreate the database, or use a subject you have not used yet.
#
# The three are checked as a set by DevAuthHeaderConventionTests: @name and @email must be
# what IntegrationFixture.DevDisplayName and DevEmail derive from the committed @subject, so a
# swapped @subject that leaves either behind fails the build instead of quietly re-creating #64.
@subject = dev-user
@name    = Dev User
@email   = dev-user@dev.auditworthy.local
@tenant  = dev
@roles   = system_admin


### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── liveness ───
# Never calls the LLM — safe to poll while waiting for a cold boot.
GET {{base}}/alive

### Dependencies (DB, Redis) reachable. Assert this too: a Development-only /health means the
### production liveness probe 404s against a perfectly healthy app.
GET {{base}}/health


### ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── platform ───
### Modules and the tabs this caller may see. Must contain `compliance` with chat + controls.
GET {{base}}/api/platform/modules
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### Who the platform thinks you are, and your effective permissions.
GET {{base}}/api/platform/me
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}


### The ADMT disclosure: what the agent did or proposed, with what human oversight. The platform
### maps this for ANY authenticated tenant member; Auditworthy narrows it to `compliance.view`,
### because in this product the rows carry register content — control reference, proposed status,
### reason text (#70, AiDecisionDisclosureGuard). Swap {{roles}} for a role holding nothing and
### this returns 403; before the guard it returned 200 with rows.
GET {{base}}/api/platform/ai-decisions
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}


### ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── compliance API ───
### The Controls tab's data endpoint — the server-driven table behind /compliance/controls.
### Requires `compliance.view`.
GET {{base}}/api/compliance/controls
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}


### ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── chat over AG-UI (SSE) ───
### A read turn. Expect: RUN_STARTED, TOOL_CALL_START(list_controls), TEXT_MESSAGE_*,
### CUSTOM(token_usage), RUN_FINISHED. No CUSTOM(approval_required).
POST {{base}}/api/agui/{{module}}
Content-Type: application/json
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

{
  "messages": [
    { "id": "m1", "role": "user", "content": "Which controls are not yet effective?" }
  ]
}

### One control in detail.
POST {{base}}/api/agui/{{module}}
Content-Type: application/json
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

{
  "messages": [
    { "id": "m1", "role": "user", "content": "Show me control A.5.1" }
  ]
}

### A WRITE. Expect CUSTOM(approval_required) and a reply that does NOT claim the status changed.
### If this ever executes straight through, the product's central claim is broken — stop and read
### RUNBOOK.md §6.
POST {{base}}/api/agui/{{module}}
Content-Type: application/json
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

{
  "messages": [
    { "id": "m1", "role": "user", "content": "Propose moving A.5.1 to Effective because the policy was approved" }
  ]
}

### The same write as compliance-reader. The tool is never offered to the model at all — RBAC runs
### BEFORE the model, so there is nothing to jailbreak. Expect no TOOL_CALL_START for it.
POST {{base}}/api/agui/{{module}}
Content-Type: application/json
X-Dev-Subject: it-reader
X-Dev-Name: It Reader
X-Dev-Email: it-reader@dev.auditworthy.local
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: compliance-reader

{
  "messages": [
    { "id": "m1", "role": "user", "content": "Propose moving A.5.1 to Effective because the policy was approved" }
  ]
}


### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── approvals ───
### What is parked, with the arguments a human is being asked to sign off. Copy an `id` into
### @approvalId below. As compliance-analyst this returns 403, by design.
GET {{base}}/api/chat/approvals
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

@approvalId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### Approve → the call RUNS, and resolves to whatever it actually did. A tool that succeeds returns
### status "Executed" with its return value in `result`. Under the Mock provider the status argument
### is always "example", so this returns 422 with the tool's validation message and resolves
### `Failed` — expected, not a defect (#29). There is no way to send valid arguments: approve takes
### no request body.
POST {{base}}/api/chat/approvals/{{approvalId}}/approve
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### Reject → nothing runs. Returns status "Rejected".
POST {{base}}/api/chat/approvals/{{approvalId}}/reject
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}


### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── conversations ───
GET {{base}}/api/chat/conversations
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}


### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── admin ───
### THE tool-wiring check. Every compliance tool must appear with its permission, and
### propose_control_change must be the only one with requiresApproval: true. A tool missing here
### is registered in the manifest but not in ComplianceToolSource (or vice versa) and is silently
### never callable.
GET {{base}}/api/admin/security/catalog
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### Role baselines as seeded from Program.cs, editable per tenant at runtime.
###
### THIS is where you assert the product's central RBAC claim: in the response,
### `compliance-analyst` must hold an enumerated allowlist with NO `chat.approvals.manage` and NO
### `tools.compliance.*` wildcard. An analyst that can clear its own gate makes the approval lane
### ceremony — see SECURITY.md.
###
### There is no per-role GET. `/api/admin/roles/{role}/permissions` exists as **PUT only** (it is
### the editor, not a reader) and returns 405 for a GET — read the list here instead.
GET {{base}}/api/admin/roles
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### Users in the tenant.
GET {{base}}/api/admin/users
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### Append-only: every tool invocation, who ran it, and whether it succeeded.
GET {{base}}/api/admin/audit/tool-calls
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### Sign-in and permission-denial events.
GET {{base}}/api/admin/audit/auth-events
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### Token usage — empty until at least one chat turn has run.
GET {{base}}/api/admin/usage?days=30
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### Per-tenant AI provider config. Keys are write-only in the vault and never appear in a response
### or in any file in this repo.
GET {{base}}/api/admin/ai-settings
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### Background jobs queued for the calling user. There is no `GET /api/jobs` collection route —
### the platform exposes `/api/jobs/mine`, `/api/jobs/{jobId}` and `/api/jobs/{jobId}/cancel`.
### Empty on this product: the compliance module declares no jobs (PLAN.md §9).
GET {{base}}/api/jobs/mine
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Name: {{name}}
X-Dev-Email: {{email}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}
